WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.757 --> 00:00:07.590
ANNA KATE HICKS: My
name is Anna Kate Hicks,

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.170
and I'm a freshman
from Cincinnati, Ohio.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.920
It is my pleasure this afternoon
to introduce our speaker.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.379
Richard B. Gunderman is
Chancellor's Professor

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:21.300
of Radiology, Pediatrics,
Medical Education, Philosophy,

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.660
Liberal Arts, Philanthropy,
and Medical Humanities

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.020
and Health Studies at
Indiana University, where

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.130
he's also the John A. Campbell
Professor of Radiology.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:36.240
He also serves as Bicentennial
Professor at Indiana University

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.800
and as President of the Medical
Staff Executive at IU Health.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.940
He received his AB from Wabash
College and his M.D. and PhD

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.230
from the University of Chicago.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:51.960
He's a 10-time recipient of
the Indiana University Trustees

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:52.860
Teaching Award.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.280
And in 2012, he received
the Glaser Teaching Award

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.060
from the Association of
American Medical Colleges.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.959
Dr. Gunderman is the author of
numerous articles and 15 books,

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:08.010
including We Make a
Life by What We Give,

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.760
Tesla, Contagion,
and Marie Curie.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.950
Please help me in
welcoming Dr. Gunderman.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.331
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:24.888 --> 00:01:26.430
RICHARD B. GUNDERMAN:
Good afternoon.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:27.720
It's a treat to be here.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.330
I didn't know what I was doing
when I applied to college,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:37.110
but I stumbled into a very
good all-male liberal arts

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.980
college in Northwest
Central Indiana called

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.090
Wabash College, actually an
all-male institution, which

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.350
was not an enticement to me.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:51.600
But I had a chance to meet
terrific faculty members who

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.060
opened up intellectual horizons
and professional opportunities

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.290
that I don't think would have
even occurred to me, let alone

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.250
seemed feasible.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:06.360
And then, as AK
indicated, thanks to them,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.419
I ended up at the
University of Chicago,

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.960
pursued fairly conventional
medical studies,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.020
but was in a program called
the Medical Scientist Training

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:21.090
Program, and got to study in
the Committee on Social Thought.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.910
So I went to an undergraduate
liberal arts institution.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.910
And if you read the
description of the Committee

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:29.130
on Social Thought at the
University of Chicago,

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:32.010
at least at the
time I was there,

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.290
it described itself
as, among other things,

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:39.310
providing a liberal education
at the graduate level.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.790
So I'm a person who's
had the opportunity

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:46.740
to pursue a liberal arts
education for a long time

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:49.440
at a few institutions.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.080
And I'm delighted to bring
some of that learning

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.320
today to this talk, which
is on the importance

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.850
of the liberal arts.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:00.900
But we'll be focusing
on a physician's view.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.480
Would a physician have
anything useful to say

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.590
about the liberal arts,
and equally interesting,

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.690
does the liberal
arts have anything

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.180
to contribute to medicine.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.840
So let me start
out by correcting

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.810
what could be a
misapprehension that

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.650
might flow from the title
of this presentation.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.260
In my view, the
liberal arts aren't

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.940
really separable from medicine,
nor are the liberal arts

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.820
preparation for medicine.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.730
In fact, the liberal
arts and medicine

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.490
are largely coextensive.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:42.060
They would overlap a
lot on a Venn diagram,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:45.700
at least if we see them clearly.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:52.690
To thrive as a reader or a
teacher or a writer of books,

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:57.490
one must attend to many of
the same things that command

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:00.840
a physician's attention.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.720
To care well for patients
requires the same habits

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:15.320
of ear, mind, and heart that the
liberal arts aim to cultivate.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:20.690
In both spheres, it takes
years, perhaps decades, perhaps

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.320
even a lifetime, to
learn to perceive,

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:30.740
understand, and respond with
compassion and imagination

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:34.250
to the human reality before us.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:38.270
Both spheres require the
cultivation of essentially

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:42.880
the same human excellences.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.360
Consider a story
shared with me by one

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:52.450
of my favorite medical students,
whose name is Muhammad.

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:57.940
Muhammad left the hospital
one dank winter night.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.180
And as he walked
to his bicycle, he

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:08.350
saw a large mountain of a
man carrying three bags,

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:12.310
approaching him on the sidewalk.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:16.300
The man's intentions
were unclear.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:21.670
Did he, for example,
plan to ask for money?

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:25.540
Did he intend to
take it by force?

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:29.050
But Muhammad resisted
the temptation

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.800
to cross to the other side.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.400
The distance between
them soon closed.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.460
And soon, the man's
chest was just

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:43.710
inches from Muhammad's face.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.620
The man said, do you work here?

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.430
I need to get to
the emergency room.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:55.610
Muhammad replied, It's
a few blocks away.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:59.960
The man asked, could
you drive me there?

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:04.940
Actually, said Muhammad,
I rode my bike today.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:08.840
The man took off his
backpack, opened it,

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.560
and handed Muhammad a blanket.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.080
He said, Here, take this.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:18.410
It's too cold to be out biking.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:23.240
Then the man said please
don't leave me here alone.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.120
I'm blind.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:31.130
If you want to learn, teach,
or write about the importance

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:36.830
of not jumping to conclusions,
not writing people off

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:42.860
based on how they look
or talk or even smell,

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:48.230
you could do worse than to read
a letter the greatest physician

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:53.270
poet in the English
language, John Keats,

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:57.650
wrote on a dank
winter night in 1817

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:02.180
to his brothers, George and Tom.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.490
Considering the
close relationship

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:08.120
of truth and beauty
in King Lear,

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:13.880
Keats describes what he
calls negative capability.

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:17.240
Negative capability--
the ability

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:23.360
to be in uncertainties,
mysteries, and doubts

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:30.260
without any irritable reaching
after fact and reason.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:33.650
When we encounter something
we're not sure about--

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:39.590
an idea, a new way of
thinking, a large mountain

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:43.960
of a man carrying three bags--

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:49.120
Keats counsels us to
observe, to be patient,

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:54.760
to dwell in uncertainty,
and to avoid resorting

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:56.880
to predetermined categories.

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:04.090
His counsel to patients
shines all the more brightly

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:09.700
when we consider that Keats
himself died of tuberculosis

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:12.235
at the age of only 25 years.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.910
Keats is like Socrates.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:23.440
If we explain every
new thing we encounter

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:27.390
in terms of what
we already know,

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:30.890
we'll never learn anything new.

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:38.490
We'll keep explaining everything
as A or B, A or not A,

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:45.670
and never recognize that
there's also a C, a D, and an E.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:48.610
Socrates was likened
by his contemporaries

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:54.920
to a torpedo fish or a
stingray, which jolts

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:58.550
its prey, disorienting it.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:02.210
In the dialogues of
Plato, Minos wants

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:06.170
to enrich his cocktail
party repertoire

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:12.200
by quoting Socrates'
accounts of the virtues.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:17.450
Simmias and Cebes want to
know why a person should

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:22.220
keep striving to be
good even though death

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:27.180
comes to the virtuous
and vicious alike.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.030
But before they can
gain real insight,

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:33.930
Socrates' interlocutors
must first

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:40.320
set aside, at least for a time,
what they think they know.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.110
If they're going to
gain new insight,

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:47.970
they must first admit
that they do not know.

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:53.190
Then they can cast off anew
in the pursuit of wisdom.

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:57.660
They must drink liberally
from that most ironic

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.830
of wellsprings,
negative capability.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.860
Reviewing video
recordings of hundreds

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:13.570
of physician-patient
encounters, a colleague of mine

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:16.840
determined that
we physicians can

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:22.870
listen to patients for how many
seconds until we interrupt?

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:27.750
The answer is 18.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:39.370
We, that is, physicians, in the
words of Sergeant Joe Friday,

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.230
we want just the facts.

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:46.430
That is just the parts of
the story that fit neatly

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:48.950
into our preconceptions.

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.800
And we wanted them to be
conveyed as efficiently

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:54.260
as possible.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:11:00.320
How easily we forget that the
patient is sharing with us more

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:04.490
than the clues to a diagnosis.

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:07.930
The patient is sharing
a human experience,

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:11.710
one that deserves to
be treated as more

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:17.150
than a mere means to an end.

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:23.200
Paraphrasing Kant,
we must never resolve

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:27.610
to treat any story as
a means to confirming

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:34.410
our own preconceptions only,
but always, at the same time,

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:39.610
as a source of
illumination in itself.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:44.290
What we need, whether taking
a history from a patient

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:50.860
or reading one of Keats' odes or
Plato's dialogues, what we need

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:54.970
is the capacity to listen
patiently, to hear what

00:11:54.970 --> 00:12:00.640
is being said, and to
allow the story itself

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:04.690
to tell us what it means
rather than making the story

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:11.000
tell us what we expect
or want to hear.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:14.780
Consider another story,
this one shared with me

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:20.330
by a priest, whose name
I can no longer recall.

00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:23.930
As part of his clinical
pastoral education,

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:28.670
he was assigned to make
monthly home visits

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:32.090
to a patient named Shirley.

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:36.350
Shirley was an elderly
woman who lived alone.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:43.010
At every visit, Shirley
would talk virtually nonstop,

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:46.340
and her visitor soon
realized he couldn't

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:49.250
get in a word edgewise.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:52.220
Feeling that he was not
contributing anything,

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:57.630
the priest began to dread
his visits with Shirley.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:02.030
She did all the talking,
and he felt useless.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:04.970
Almost as soon as
he sat down, he

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:07.970
found himself looking
for opportunities

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:11.250
to say that he needed to go.

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:15.320
Some time later,
after Shirley died,

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:19.790
the priest received a
letter from his supervisor

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:25.010
in which Shirley went on
at length about how much

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:28.240
his visits had meant to her.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:32.420
The priest thought
he was doing nothing,

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:36.470
but by being there and
listening to Shirley,

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:39.830
he was providing
exactly what she needed.

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:46.430
In this, the priest
is recalling a lesson

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:52.270
of the greatest physician
dramatist in world literature,

00:13:52.270 --> 00:13:55.070
Anton Chekhov.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:14:00.500
Chekhov practiced medicine
all his adult life, right up

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:04.370
to his premature death
from tuberculosis

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:07.850
at the age of 44 years.

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:11.690
In his short story,
"Yearning," we

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:17.530
are treated to a ringside
seat on human loneliness.

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:23.860
A poor cab man, a taxi driver,
we might say, named Iona,

00:14:23.860 --> 00:14:28.360
tries to share with his fares
what is weighing heavily

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:30.510
on his heart.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:34.380
His son, who should
have succeeded him,

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:38.040
has recently died of
a febrile illness,

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:42.260
leaving the old man all alone.

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:45.950
First Iona reaches out
to a military officer

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.380
intent on reaching
his destination

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:50.990
as efficiently as possible.

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:54.740
Then he reaches out to a group
of three drunken young men

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:57.020
looking for a good time.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:00.650
Then he reaches out
to a fellow cab man.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:05.270
And finally, he's left
with his own mare,

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:10.690
who seems to listen
as she munches on hay.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:15.820
He says to her, now, suppose
you had a little colt.

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:22.380
And suppose that little colt,
all at once, went and died.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:27.070
You'd be sorry, wouldn't you?

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:30.970
The practice of medicine, like
the study of great literature,

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:36.070
reminds us that sometimes
what we human beings most need

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:41.260
is someone to listen,
someone just to be there.

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:43.870
Recall the Book of Job.

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:47.600
Job, the greatest
man in all the East,

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:52.130
righteous and upright before
God, has lost everything--

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:56.270
his property, his
children, his own health.

00:15:56.270 --> 00:16:00.380
He's fallen as far as a human
being can possibly descend.

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:07.220
And he sits in ashes, scratching
himself with pot shards.

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:11.660
When his three friends,
Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:16.520
arrive on the scene, they
resist the temptation

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:21.710
to launch into sophisticated,
philosophical, and theological

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:28.700
explanations or justifications
for what has befallen Job.

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:34.040
They simply sit down silently
at their friend's side,

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:39.400
remaining there for seven
days and seven nights.

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:43.150
To be sure, they will
later stray from this path,

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:50.850
but their first impulse is
their best, simply to be there.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:58.710
We can't fix poverty, ignorance,
injustice, or terminal illness.

00:16:58.710 --> 00:17:03.660
We certainly cannot
fix mortality.

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:09.030
But we can recognize our
part and play it well.

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:14.579
Not the part of the immortal
and ageless gods, whose lives

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:17.339
in Homer sometimes
resemble nothing

00:17:17.339 --> 00:17:21.040
more than daytime
television drama,

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:26.390
but the human part, the part of
Achilles and Priam and Hector,

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:29.090
whose only hope
for coming to life

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:33.530
lies in recognition of
their shared humanity.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:37.460
Achilles must see
his father in Priam,

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:41.990
and Priam must see
his son in Achilles.

00:17:41.990 --> 00:17:45.050
Achilles must see
himself in Hector,

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:50.090
and Hector must see himself
loaded up in battle gear

00:17:50.090 --> 00:17:53.900
through the eyes of
his terrified son.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:57.680
It is our capacity to
be there, in the sense

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:00.950
of occupying someone
else's space,

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:05.870
inhabiting their reality
that constitutes one

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:09.530
of humanity's saving graces.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:12.600
When we see through
the eyes of another,

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:18.900
we gain a more complete
vision of the human situation.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:22.470
Like many academic hospitals
and health systems,

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:24.750
in Indianapolis,
we have a program

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:29.760
called No One Dies Alone.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.490
Its purpose is not
to sustain a failing

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:37.660
pulse or respiration
for as long as possible

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:41.520
but to recognize the
inevitability and even

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:48.180
the appropriateness of dying
and trying to enable people

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:51.190
to die as well as possible.

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:53.850
There are good deaths
and bad deaths,

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:57.630
and often one of the
physician's most urgent missions

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:01.470
is to recognize and
respect this difference

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:05.640
and work on behalf
of patient and family

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:08.025
to midwife a good death.

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:15.750
This means addressing one of the
most common fears I encounter

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:18.390
with patients about dying--

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:21.290
dying alone.

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:26.590
Volunteers simply sit by the
bedside of dying patients.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:30.760
Sometimes they hear nothing
but aginal respirations,

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:34.120
but other times,
they hear stories.

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:38.530
And sometimes the stories
are rather like those

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:42.490
of Chekhov's cab man.

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:46.360
When they listen with eyes
and ears and heart open,

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:53.260
they are granted the opportunity
to answer the human call.

00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:57.160
Consider another story,
this one shared with me

00:19:57.160 --> 00:20:02.150
by the daughter of a
man deceased long ago.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.000
In the first half
of the 20th century,

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:09.890
Otis Bowen M.D. Grew
up the son of a teacher

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:12.620
in Northwest Indiana.

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:17.750
Otis Bowen's father had
eight grades of students

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:23.300
in a one-room school, where he
functioned as teacher, janitor,

00:20:23.300 --> 00:20:28.520
and nurse, carrying wood and
water to the schoolhouse,

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:33.040
and earning $2.50 a day.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:36.310
Bowen decided to
follow a different path

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:38.710
and become a physician.

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:41.740
To get money for college
and medical school,

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:46.900
he hoed potatoes
for $0.10 an hour.

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:52.600
He milked a cow morning
and night for $0.10 a day.

00:20:52.600 --> 00:21:00.700
He mowed grass for $0.25 a
yard, $0.35 for large yards.

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:06.930
And he shucked oats
and wheat for $1 a day.

00:21:06.930 --> 00:21:10.890
Bowen persevered,
eventually graduating

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:13.860
from the Indiana University
School of Medicine,

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:17.970
serving in the Pacific
Theater in World War II,

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:22.410
setting up his own
practice in rural Indiana,

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:27.790
delivering 3,000 babies over
the course of his career,

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:31.530
including five
generations in one family,

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:36.030
and attending all of
his patients' funerals.

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:40.440
He also won election to
the state legislature,

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:43.680
served as Speaker of
the House of the Indiana

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:48.450
House of Representatives, became
the first governor in Indiana

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:51.780
to serve eight
consecutive years,

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:57.540
and then served as US Secretary
of Health and Human Services

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:00.570
during the Reagan
administration.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:06.720
Otis Bowen was known as Doc
throughout his adult life.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:08.670
And over the course
of his career,

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:11.880
he found himself devoting
more and more of his time

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:16.440
to public service
and less to medicine.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:20.940
Once, though, relatively
late in his life, a woman

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:23.010
came up to him.

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:25.800
She reminded him
of the days when,

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.680
much earlier in
his medical career,

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:34.080
he had served as coroner
of Marshall County.

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:39.630
She called to his mind a case he
had been summoned to one day--

00:22:39.630 --> 00:22:46.770
a middle-aged man killed
in an industrial accident.

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.170
She reminded him
that he had performed

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:52.930
the autopsy on that man.

00:22:52.930 --> 00:22:56.650
And she told him that the
man on whom he had performed

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:59.505
the autopsy was her father.

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:05.890
Otis Bowen recalled the
event in vivid detail,

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:11.410
describing what had happened and
how deeply it had touched him.

00:23:11.410 --> 00:23:18.150
And as he talked, tears
streamed down his face.

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:21.990
In recalling this encounter
with the mortal remains

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:25.050
of another human
being, I suspect

00:23:25.050 --> 00:23:29.040
Bowen had encountered
his father's mortality,

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:35.040
his own mortality, the
mortality of humankind.

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:37.620
Bowen had developed
personal relationships

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:44.670
with famous people, including
US presidents, vice presidents,

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:48.750
speakers of the house,
senate majority leaders.

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:51.150
He moved easily
among the wealthy,

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:56.480
who sought to curry the favor
of government officials.

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:59.690
He achieved unprecedented
political success

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:03.410
in his home state and
assumed responsibility

00:24:03.410 --> 00:24:07.810
for the largest department
in the federal government.

00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:12.050
Otis Bowen was, in
short, a big deal,

00:24:12.050 --> 00:24:16.040
and his calls would be taken
by the most famous, richest,

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:20.080
and powerful people
in our country.

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:25.230
But what really moved him,
what brought him to tears,

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:28.530
was not seeing his
name on the front page

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:34.440
or securing a financial
windfall or winning high office.

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:37.620
What really touched him
was an encounter with two

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:39.270
human beings--

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:45.540
one long dead, and the other
the decedent's offspring.

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:52.110
It was akin to the encounter
between Achilles and Priam.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:54.750
Such encounters are the
distinctive privilege

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:57.840
of physicians, who
are called upon

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:03.570
not merely to pronounce
death or assign it a cause

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:11.120
but also to witness it
and feel its significance.

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:15.530
We adopt disguises,
we human beings.

00:25:15.530 --> 00:25:22.160
We load ourselves up with
titles, riches, authority.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:26.510
Yet no matter who one
of us happens to be

00:25:26.510 --> 00:25:32.000
or would like respecters of
persons to think that we are,

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:35.270
underneath, we are all human.

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:39.110
Even the most famous,
richest, and most powerful

00:25:39.110 --> 00:25:42.110
people are made
of the same flesh

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:48.570
and blood as the weakest, the
poorest, and the most obscure.

00:25:48.570 --> 00:25:54.300
They too wailed as infants,
suckled at their mother's

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:59.030
breast, bounced on
their father's knee,

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:04.670
railed during school years
against the cafeteria food,

00:26:04.670 --> 00:26:10.250
got butterflies in their stomach
on their first date, debated

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:16.820
the care of aging parents, and
before long, found themselves

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:20.690
the subject of debate.

00:26:20.690 --> 00:26:23.830
Our costumes
present one reality.

00:26:23.830 --> 00:26:29.560
But to examine us, to know
us, and to care for us,

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:33.610
the physician must
allow such layers

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:39.760
to fall away until the
unadorned human being underneath

00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:41.760
is revealed.

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:46.800
The physician's job is
not to dismiss or condemn.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:50.250
The physician's job
is to know and care.

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:55.100
Much the same could
be said for the job

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:59.120
of the poet, the
philosopher, the theologian.

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:03.410
Consider one of the greatest
novellas ever penned,

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:08.210
Leo Tolstoy's The
Death of Ivan Ilyich.

00:27:08.210 --> 00:27:11.420
In it, a successful
lawyer and judge

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:16.550
who has devoted his life
to moving up the ladder,

00:27:16.550 --> 00:27:20.630
maintaining a decorous
life, and doing

00:27:20.630 --> 00:27:24.510
what is expected
by those above him,

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:27.440
he develops a serious illness.

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:30.380
As he wrestles with
his own mortality,

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:33.470
he discovers that
he's not only dying

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:40.140
from what I presumed to be a
cancer of the digestive system,

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:45.540
he discovers that he's been
dying his whole life, even

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:51.290
during his prime, because
he has never really lived.

00:27:51.290 --> 00:27:54.380
In fact, he has
known less and less

00:27:54.380 --> 00:27:59.750
of life's meaning and purpose
with each passing year.

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:02.120
The things that
brought him pleasure--

00:28:02.120 --> 00:28:08.270
house, car, job title,
wealth, prestige--

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:10.940
they no longer
bring him pleasure.

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:14.660
And he feels himself caught
up in a web of deception

00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:18.990
from which he has no means
of extricating himself.

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:23.660
It's not merely that no one will
tell him what he wants to hear,

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:29.000
but that no one will
utter the truth.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:34.430
You don't want to end up in
the position of Ivan Ilyich,

00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:39.350
who in the last days of his
life finds himself tortured

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:44.810
by a thought that he
cannot get out of its mind.

00:28:44.810 --> 00:28:48.200
It occurred to Ivan Ilyich that
what had appeared perfectly

00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:52.760
impossible before,
namely that he has not

00:28:52.760 --> 00:28:58.680
spent his life as he should have
done, might after all be true.

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:02.580
It occurred to him that his
scarcely perceptible attempts

00:29:02.580 --> 00:29:06.480
to struggle against
what was considered good

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:13.760
by the most highly
placed of his associates,

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:15.770
he had immediately suppressed.

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:25.220
What he had been
looking for was false.

00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:29.090
His life had been based
on a false premise.

00:29:29.090 --> 00:29:32.210
His professional duties and the
whole arrangement of his life

00:29:32.210 --> 00:29:36.680
and of his family and all his
social and official interests

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:39.380
might all have been false.

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:42.380
He tried to defend
those things to himself

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:47.620
and suddenly felt the weakness
of what he was defending.

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:50.755
There was nothing to defend.

00:29:50.755 --> 00:29:54.210
Ivan Ilyich is truly lost.

00:29:54.210 --> 00:29:57.240
He's positioned himself
at the periphery

00:29:57.240 --> 00:29:59.940
of the human solar system.

00:29:59.940 --> 00:30:03.420
He's been chasing
after shiny things

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:07.920
while turning his back on
the true source of warmth

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.880
and light.

00:30:09.880 --> 00:30:13.390
His physicians, like him,
have expunged everything

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:17.950
real and human from their
purely technical practice,

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:19.630
and they're not much help.

00:30:19.630 --> 00:30:23.650
They debate diagnoses
and treatment regimens

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:28.730
while the human being under
their care withers away.

00:30:28.730 --> 00:30:30.650
Yet they could help.

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:34.160
They could recognize that
Ivan Ilyich is dying.

00:30:34.160 --> 00:30:37.910
They could treat him not
as a malfunctioning organ,

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:42.860
but as a human being, a creature
who, regardless of his age,

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:48.650
needs someone to be near, to
talk, to listen, and to care.

00:30:48.650 --> 00:30:52.040
Ivan Ilyich is a reminder
that the best things in life

00:30:52.040 --> 00:30:53.480
are priceless.

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:58.940
That they are almost never
represented on resumes or CVs,

00:30:58.940 --> 00:31:03.080
and that we cannot
take them with us.

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:07.910
Yet we can share them with
others, and in so doing,

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:14.720
not diminish but actually
enrich our own supply.

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:19.250
For it is in sharing,
something Ivan Ilyich

00:31:19.250 --> 00:31:23.630
has never done, that the
things that really matter

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:26.600
come to life.

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:29.960
These are the physician's
most precious resources--

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:34.070
time to spend where the
return on investment

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:41.180
is greatest, attention to devote
to the things that matter most,

00:31:41.180 --> 00:31:46.430
beauty, which lies all around
us if we have eyes to see

00:31:46.430 --> 00:31:51.680
and ears to hear, truth, a
glimpse into the way things

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:55.790
really are, and
love, the capacity

00:31:55.790 --> 00:32:00.860
to share a moment or a
lifetime with another person

00:32:00.860 --> 00:32:04.610
in grieving or
rejoicing with them.

00:32:04.610 --> 00:32:09.920
Ivan Ilyich is not a protagonist
to admire or emulate.

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:13.800
He is a portrait of a
human life misspent,

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:18.120
a reminder that even those
with their fingers on the pulse

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:23.100
of life itself, physicians and
other health professionals,

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:26.880
can become dangerously
distracted from what

00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:30.310
most deserves our attention.

00:32:30.310 --> 00:32:37.270
Diagnosing, prognosing, curing,
palliating, comforting--

00:32:37.270 --> 00:32:42.610
these activities work in service
to a deeper human reality,

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:45.070
one that reveals a
glimpse of itself

00:32:45.070 --> 00:32:50.940
every time a baby is
born or a person dies.

00:32:50.940 --> 00:32:55.560
We must do our jobs well but
always mindful of a deeper

00:32:55.560 --> 00:33:00.900
reality, a thread that Ivan
Ilyich completely lost contact

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:02.830
with.

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:05.530
Consider another story.

00:33:05.530 --> 00:33:08.800
A woman with advanced
head and neck cancer

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:14.620
was scheduled to undergo
radical surgery the next day.

00:33:14.620 --> 00:33:18.010
She had led a life that
many on her medical team

00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:20.630
did not approve of.

00:33:20.630 --> 00:33:24.710
After the team visited her
room and obtained her informed

00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:28.910
consent, they prepared to exit.

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:31.760
But before they did so,
she handed each one of them

00:33:31.760 --> 00:33:37.290
a small plastic bag
containing a mustard seed.

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:40.170
The members of the
team turned and exited

00:33:40.170 --> 00:33:44.060
in orders of seniority.

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:49.140
As the attending surgeon did
so, he dropped the mustard seed

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:51.660
in the trash.

00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:56.430
Then the head and neck
fellow followed suit,

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:58.510
discarding the gift.

00:33:58.510 --> 00:34:01.830
Then the senior surgery
resident, and so on, right down

00:34:01.830 --> 00:34:04.860
the line, until the third
year medical student,

00:34:04.860 --> 00:34:08.610
the junior person on
the team, left the room.

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:11.670
But this time, instead
of disposing of it,

00:34:11.670 --> 00:34:15.480
the most junior team member
tucked the little plastic bag

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:18.820
into the pocket
of his white coat.

00:34:18.820 --> 00:34:22.630
His act presents
us with questions.

00:34:22.630 --> 00:34:26.020
What did the patient
mean by handing

00:34:26.020 --> 00:34:29.050
the members of the
team plastic bags

00:34:29.050 --> 00:34:32.080
with mustard seeds in them?

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:34.989
What did they mean to
her, and what did they

00:34:34.989 --> 00:34:37.330
mean to the team members?

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:40.630
And why did the medical
student, the most junior person

00:34:40.630 --> 00:34:47.019
on the team, break ranks and
keep his mustard seed gift?

00:34:49.980 --> 00:34:53.670
Thomas Browne, the
greatest physician essayist

00:34:53.670 --> 00:34:58.380
in the English language, knew
the answer to that question.

00:34:58.380 --> 00:35:01.770
In his masterpiece, The
Religion of a Physician,

00:35:01.770 --> 00:35:06.710
composed in the 17th
century, he reminds us

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:12.320
of truths that have not dimmed
a lumen in the long interval

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:14.680
since.

00:35:14.680 --> 00:35:21.480
Those centuries saw a lot, since
the 17th century in medicine.

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:24.180
Since then we've seen the
discovery or introduction

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:27.510
of blood cells,
bacteria, the use

00:35:27.510 --> 00:35:30.180
of citrus fruits
to prevent scurvy,

00:35:30.180 --> 00:35:34.710
vaccination against smallpox,
inhalation anesthesia,

00:35:34.710 --> 00:35:39.900
the stethoscope, antisepsis
to prevent infection, the germ

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:46.350
theory, X-rays,
penicillin, blood typing,

00:35:46.350 --> 00:35:50.670
electrocardiography,
chemotherapy for cancer,

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:53.490
the double helical
structure of DNA,

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:58.920
organ transplantation, CT
scanners, MRI scanners,

00:35:58.920 --> 00:36:02.490
the eradication of smallpox
from the face of the Earth,

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:07.440
kidney dialysis, and vaccination
as a means of preventing

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:08.370
cancer.

00:36:08.370 --> 00:36:10.740
Wow.

00:36:10.740 --> 00:36:12.630
There was a lot
that Thomas Browne

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:17.230
didn't know that we do know.

00:36:17.230 --> 00:36:20.920
And yet Thomas Browne
glimpsed some eternal truths

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:24.550
that we all too
easily lose sight of.

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:27.490
Consider Browne's
humanity, which

00:36:27.490 --> 00:36:32.240
is to say his
charity, as expressed

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:35.480
in The Religion of a Physician.

00:36:35.480 --> 00:36:40.850
"It is a barbarous part of
inhumanity to add unto any

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:48.100
afflicted party's misery or
endeavor to multiply in any man

00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:54.470
a passion whose single nature
is already above his patient's.

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:57.430
This was the greatest
affliction of Job,

00:36:57.430 --> 00:37:01.330
and those oblique
ex-postulations of his friends

00:37:01.330 --> 00:37:07.540
a deeper injury than the
downright blows of the Devil.

00:37:07.540 --> 00:37:12.490
It is not the tears of our own
eyes only, but of our friends

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:18.040
also that do exhaust the
current of our sorrows, which

00:37:18.040 --> 00:37:22.450
falling into many streams,
run more peaceably

00:37:22.450 --> 00:37:26.860
and is contented with
a narrower channel.

00:37:26.860 --> 00:37:30.070
It is an act within
the power of charity

00:37:30.070 --> 00:37:35.260
to translate a passion out
of one breast into another

00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:40.870
and to divide a sorrow
almost out of itself.

00:37:40.870 --> 00:37:44.140
For an affliction,
like a dimension,

00:37:44.140 --> 00:37:49.420
may be so divided, as if
not indivisible, at least

00:37:49.420 --> 00:37:52.920
to become insensible.

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:57.180
Now, with my friend I desire
not to share or participate

00:37:57.180 --> 00:38:02.080
but to engross his sorrows,
that by making them

00:38:02.080 --> 00:38:06.570
mine own I may more
easily discuss them.

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:11.430
For in mine own reason and
within myself I can command

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:16.740
that which I cannot entreat
without myself and within

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:20.220
the circle of another."

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:23.040
The liberal arts don't
merely inform the practice

00:38:23.040 --> 00:38:26.520
of the physician Thomas Browne.

00:38:26.520 --> 00:38:29.730
The physician Thomas
Browne does not merely

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:32.700
draw on the liberal arts.

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:36.840
It would be truer to say that
Thomas Browne is a perfection

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:38.490
of the liberal arts.

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:41.100
And it is through
his care of patients

00:38:41.100 --> 00:38:45.060
that this perfection
has taken place.

00:38:45.060 --> 00:38:49.560
The first Hippocratic
injunction is not merely a rule

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:53.700
that he chooses to follow,
but a habit deeply ingrained

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:56.640
in the fibers of his being--

00:38:56.640 --> 00:39:00.300
do no harm.

00:39:00.300 --> 00:39:03.660
To reach out with compassion
to those who are suffering

00:39:03.660 --> 00:39:06.510
has become second nature to him.

00:39:06.510 --> 00:39:09.810
He knows, as he knows
that an apple will fall,

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:13.860
that if he shares another's
pain, the burden of that pain

00:39:13.860 --> 00:39:15.720
will be lightened.

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:20.460
He is not merely recognizing,
acknowledging, classifying,

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:24.690
forecasting, and treating
another's affliction.

00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:27.540
He is encompassing it.

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:30.690
By giving others the opportunity
to share what they usually

00:39:30.690 --> 00:39:34.500
keep to themselves,
he creates conditions

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:38.430
under which trust can thrive.

00:39:38.430 --> 00:39:42.120
He is finding within the
practice of an ancient healing

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:48.240
art not merely an opportunity
to divert the path of a disease,

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:52.060
but to forge a friendship.

00:39:52.060 --> 00:39:56.620
Browne is most assuredly
a physician, a gentleman,

00:39:56.620 --> 00:40:03.930
and a scholar, but he is, above
all, a human being in full.

00:40:03.930 --> 00:40:06.330
Consider a much
more recent portrait

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:09.660
of the physician
as a friend of man,

00:40:09.660 --> 00:40:14.790
that of the pseudonymous Dr.
John Sassall in John Berger

00:40:14.790 --> 00:40:20.310
and Jean Mohr's 1967
book, A Fortunate Man.

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:24.810
Sassall practices in Western
England's Forest of Dean.

00:40:24.810 --> 00:40:26.820
And, in many ways,
he differs sharply

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:30.270
from the people and the
community he cares for.

00:40:30.270 --> 00:40:33.600
He's different in birthplace,
socioeconomic class,

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:37.110
educational attainment,
life experience.

00:40:37.110 --> 00:40:41.580
Sassall knows that in order
to care for his neighbors,

00:40:41.580 --> 00:40:45.330
he must become one of
them, and he does this

00:40:45.330 --> 00:40:47.950
by building something.

00:40:47.950 --> 00:40:50.440
There's a castle
moat in town that

00:40:50.440 --> 00:40:54.440
has long been used as a dump.

00:40:54.440 --> 00:40:58.430
Sassall proposes that they
turn it into a garden.

00:40:58.430 --> 00:41:03.680
He gets himself named the
chair of the garden committee,

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:06.320
but he defers to
his neighbors, who

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:10.400
know a good deal more than he
about how to design and build

00:41:10.400 --> 00:41:12.340
a garden.

00:41:12.340 --> 00:41:15.700
Eventually, after years
of effort, during which he

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:18.970
has labored at their side
most summer evenings,

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:22.000
the garden is complete.

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:25.360
A neighbor, Sassall
realizes, is one

00:41:25.360 --> 00:41:29.440
with whom I have labored side
by side, working toward a shared

00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:31.090
vision.

00:41:31.090 --> 00:41:35.680
Not primarily through medical
care, but through shared work,

00:41:35.680 --> 00:41:40.900
he ceases to be merely for
them and lives among them,

00:41:40.900 --> 00:41:44.500
becoming one of them.

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:47.890
Cicero famously said, if you
have a garden and a library,

00:41:47.890 --> 00:41:50.680
you have just about everything.

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:53.350
Thomas Browne builds a library.

00:41:53.350 --> 00:41:56.180
John Sassall builds a garden.

00:41:56.180 --> 00:41:59.930
But the library is not just
a place of instruction,

00:41:59.930 --> 00:42:02.330
and the garden is
not just a source

00:42:02.330 --> 00:42:04.940
of nourishing vegetables.

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:08.210
In Brown's essays, we
find objects every bit

00:42:08.210 --> 00:42:13.020
as rich and beautiful
as a juicy red tomato.

00:42:13.020 --> 00:42:17.460
And in Sassall's garden lie
lessons that every physician,

00:42:17.460 --> 00:42:20.190
and to a lesser extent,
every human being,

00:42:20.190 --> 00:42:22.980
is called upon to learn.

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:26.640
In both cases, we
must put asunder

00:42:26.640 --> 00:42:30.570
the things that should
be separated and meld

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:33.330
the things that
should be joined,

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:39.240
all in the service of
healing, of making whole.

00:42:39.240 --> 00:42:41.970
Browne's prose and
Sassall's garden

00:42:41.970 --> 00:42:43.800
teach us the
meaning of patience,

00:42:43.800 --> 00:42:48.550
discipline, nurturance,
resilience, and hope.

00:42:48.550 --> 00:42:53.890
They teach us that wisdom
is not so much propositional

00:42:53.890 --> 00:42:58.630
as experiential, and that
the essential experience

00:42:58.630 --> 00:43:01.660
is to recognize ourselves
as part of something

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:05.770
larger, deeper,
and more enduring,

00:43:05.770 --> 00:43:10.720
something we can either
ignore or embrace, thwart

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:12.970
or cultivate.

00:43:12.970 --> 00:43:15.400
At some point, the
most fitting response

00:43:15.400 --> 00:43:20.980
is not to beat our breasts,
but to bow our heads.

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:23.560
To see the connection between
the liberal arts and medicine,

00:43:23.560 --> 00:43:26.380
we can turn to no
finer recent guide

00:43:26.380 --> 00:43:31.060
than the greatest American
physician poet, William Carlos

00:43:31.060 --> 00:43:33.280
Williams.

00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:35.290
In a chapter of
his autobiography,

00:43:35.290 --> 00:43:37.690
called The Practice,
Williams shows us

00:43:37.690 --> 00:43:42.370
that doctoring and writing,
medicine and the liberal arts,

00:43:42.370 --> 00:43:45.910
are twin aspects of
the same calling.

00:43:45.910 --> 00:43:47.920
Of his encounters
with patients, he

00:43:47.920 --> 00:43:56.640
writes, "I lost myself in the
very properties of their minds.

00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:59.070
For the moment, at
least, I actually

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:02.880
became them, whoever
they should be,

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:05.730
so that when I detached
myself from them

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:09.750
at the end of a half an hour
of intense concentration

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:12.990
over some illness which
was affecting them,

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:18.480
it was as though I were
reawakening from a sleep.

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:21.900
For the moment, I
myself did not exist.

00:44:21.900 --> 00:44:24.990
Nothing of myself affected me.

00:44:24.990 --> 00:44:28.470
As a consequence, I
came back to myself, as

00:44:28.470 --> 00:44:32.070
from any other sleep, rested.

00:44:32.070 --> 00:44:35.110
They naively asked,
how do you do it?

00:44:35.110 --> 00:44:38.350
How can you carry on an
active practice like that

00:44:38.350 --> 00:44:41.340
and at the same time
find time to write?

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:44.230
You must be superhuman.

00:44:44.230 --> 00:44:46.120
You must have, at
the very least,

00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:49.040
the energy of two people.

00:44:49.040 --> 00:44:52.750
But they do not grasp that
one occupation complements

00:44:52.750 --> 00:44:56.830
the other, that they are
two parts of the whole,

00:44:56.830 --> 00:45:01.450
that it is not two jobs at
all, that one rests the man

00:45:01.450 --> 00:45:04.000
if the other fatigues him.

00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:05.470
Forget writing.

00:45:05.470 --> 00:45:07.480
It's a trivial matter.

00:45:07.480 --> 00:45:11.830
But day in and day out, when
the inarticulate patient

00:45:11.830 --> 00:45:16.030
struggles to lay himself
bare for you, or with nothing

00:45:16.030 --> 00:45:20.890
more than a boil on his back
is so caught off balance

00:45:20.890 --> 00:45:25.750
that he reveals a whole
community's way of thought,

00:45:25.750 --> 00:45:29.170
a man is suddenly seized
again with the desire

00:45:29.170 --> 00:45:33.190
to speak of the underground
stream which, for a moment,

00:45:33.190 --> 00:45:36.220
has just come up under surface.

00:45:36.220 --> 00:45:38.500
We catch a glimpse of
something, from time

00:45:38.500 --> 00:45:42.010
to time, which shows us
that a presence has just

00:45:42.010 --> 00:45:45.910
brushed past us,
some rare thing, just

00:45:45.910 --> 00:45:50.470
when the smiling little
Italian woman has left us.

00:45:50.470 --> 00:45:54.050
For a moment, we are dazzled.

00:45:54.050 --> 00:45:55.900
What was that?

00:45:55.900 --> 00:45:57.580
We can't name it.

00:45:57.580 --> 00:46:01.870
We know it never gets into
any recognizable avenue

00:46:01.870 --> 00:46:03.790
of expression.

00:46:03.790 --> 00:46:09.010
Men will be long dead before
they can have so much as ever

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:10.870
approached it.

00:46:10.870 --> 00:46:14.530
Whole lives are spent in
the tremendous affairs

00:46:14.530 --> 00:46:19.060
of daily events without ever
approaching the great insights

00:46:19.060 --> 00:46:21.130
I see every day.

00:46:21.130 --> 00:46:24.040
We begin to see that the
underlying meaning of all

00:46:24.040 --> 00:46:28.180
they want to tell us and have
always failed to communicate

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:33.340
is the poem, the poem
which their whole lives are

00:46:33.340 --> 00:46:36.800
being lived to realize.

00:46:36.800 --> 00:46:39.070
No one will believe it.

00:46:39.070 --> 00:46:43.210
And it is the actual words
as we hear them spoken,

00:46:43.210 --> 00:46:46.770
under all circumstances
which contain it.

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:50.890
It is actually there,
in the life before us,

00:46:50.890 --> 00:46:53.470
every minute that
we are listening,

00:46:53.470 --> 00:46:57.550
a rarest element not
in our imaginations,

00:46:57.550 --> 00:47:01.570
but there, there in fact.

00:47:01.570 --> 00:47:06.610
It is that essence which is
hidden in the very words which

00:47:06.610 --> 00:47:11.600
are going in at our ears and
from which we must recover

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:16.720
underlying meaning as
realistically as we recover

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:20.250
metal out of ore."

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:21.840
And here we come to the end.

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:32.560
If you care about money,
perhaps go into business.

00:47:32.560 --> 00:47:38.970
If you care about power,
perhaps go into politics.

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:44.400
If you care about fame,
become an entertainer.

00:47:44.400 --> 00:47:50.520
But if, as Dostoevsky puts
it, you want not millions,

00:47:50.520 --> 00:47:55.230
but answers to your
questions, and especially

00:47:55.230 --> 00:48:00.500
if you want to learn to
pose even better questions,

00:48:00.500 --> 00:48:07.160
if, in other words, what you
want above all is the truth,

00:48:07.160 --> 00:48:11.600
consider a career in
the health professions.

00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:17.060
The truth lies not in
textbooks or Journal articles

00:48:17.060 --> 00:48:23.540
and certainly not in the answers
to standardized, high-stakes

00:48:23.540 --> 00:48:27.500
multiple choice tests questions.

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:30.620
The truth lies in the words
of human beings stripped

00:48:30.620 --> 00:48:34.680
bare, confronted
by life and death,

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:37.790
ordinary people,
who sometimes quite

00:48:37.790 --> 00:48:40.760
unknowingly capture
what most needs

00:48:40.760 --> 00:48:46.930
to be said in words
of unsurpassed beauty.

00:48:46.930 --> 00:48:50.480
The liberal arts are
meant to set us free,

00:48:50.480 --> 00:48:55.220
but they do so by
means of the true.

00:48:55.220 --> 00:49:00.050
And to be free we must
encounter the truth.

00:49:00.050 --> 00:49:03.320
It is perhaps in
medicine, more than just

00:49:03.320 --> 00:49:08.390
about any other calling,
that the truth of humankind

00:49:08.390 --> 00:49:11.020
is exposed.

00:49:11.020 --> 00:49:19.490
As Tolstoy said, "We find
truth as we purify gold--

00:49:19.490 --> 00:49:25.110
not by adding to it,
but by washing away all

00:49:25.110 --> 00:49:28.080
that is not gold.

00:49:28.080 --> 00:49:31.590
At its best, the
practice of medicine

00:49:31.590 --> 00:49:36.480
represents both the calling
and the consummation

00:49:36.480 --> 00:49:41.950
for which the liberal
arts have always yearned."

00:49:41.950 --> 00:49:42.957
Thank you.

00:49:42.957 --> 00:49:46.436
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:53.240 --> 00:49:54.990
ANNA KATE HICKS: Thank
you, Dr. Gunderman.

00:49:54.990 --> 00:49:58.602
We now have time for Q&amp;A,
so if you have questions--

00:50:14.550 --> 00:50:17.400
AUDIENCE: --their patients
within the clinical setting.

00:50:17.400 --> 00:50:21.330
And you were talking about
how a physician, according

00:50:21.330 --> 00:50:25.110
to Thomas Browne, how
they should encompass what

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:27.810
they are going through in
order to actually have a better

00:50:27.810 --> 00:50:29.563
relationship with that patient.

00:50:29.563 --> 00:50:31.230
So I just wondering,
what can physicians

00:50:31.230 --> 00:50:34.710
do, since they have such a
limited time in their practice,

00:50:34.710 --> 00:50:37.035
to really connect
with their patients?

00:50:40.238 --> 00:50:42.030
RICHARD B. GUNDERMAN:
The 18 seconds is not

00:50:42.030 --> 00:50:45.600
how long we have for a clinical
encounter with a patient,

00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:48.750
but the amount of time
we listen to a patient,

00:50:48.750 --> 00:50:51.690
on average, before
we interrupt them.

00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:55.440
But the spirit of your
question is bang on.

00:50:55.440 --> 00:50:59.430
The amount of time we have per
patient has been declining.

00:50:59.430 --> 00:51:03.990
Maybe an office visit was once
20 minutes, then 15 minutes,

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:05.370
now 12 minutes.

00:51:05.370 --> 00:51:08.010
What will it take to
shave it down to eight?

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:11.220
And it's not just a matter
of diminishing time.

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:14.100
It's a matter of distraction.

00:51:14.100 --> 00:51:16.020
Because when you
encounter a patient,

00:51:16.020 --> 00:51:19.860
you're encouraged
not only to listen

00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:22.740
to the patient, maybe not
even primarily to listen

00:51:22.740 --> 00:51:25.200
to the patient, but to
be thinking about things

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:28.980
like an ICD-10 code.

00:51:28.980 --> 00:51:32.250
Anybody know what
an ICD-10 code is?

00:51:32.250 --> 00:51:36.090
ICD-10 stands for the
International Classification

00:51:36.090 --> 00:51:39.010
of Diseases.

00:51:39.010 --> 00:51:42.670
I have to categorize this
patient's complaints, symptoms,

00:51:42.670 --> 00:51:44.350
signs.

00:51:44.350 --> 00:51:46.180
Otherwise, the
visit doesn't count.

00:51:46.180 --> 00:51:52.330
CPT codes, Current
Procedural Terminology--

00:51:52.330 --> 00:51:56.170
that's what counts
in health care today.

00:51:56.170 --> 00:52:00.670
If you want to learn what's
most worth caring about,

00:52:00.670 --> 00:52:04.960
you need to spend some
time in situations that

00:52:04.960 --> 00:52:07.330
draw your attention elsewhere.

00:52:07.330 --> 00:52:12.400
Because it's only in a
deficit of what counts most

00:52:12.400 --> 00:52:15.040
that I think we can
see most clearly what

00:52:15.040 --> 00:52:16.600
really counts for the most.

00:52:16.600 --> 00:52:22.630
So I come to you not as an
emissary of a profession that

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:24.010
is thriving.

00:52:24.010 --> 00:52:27.340
In many respects,
we're at the pinnacle

00:52:27.340 --> 00:52:28.810
of the history of medicine.

00:52:28.810 --> 00:52:31.420
In terms of the
diagnostic tests we do,

00:52:31.420 --> 00:52:34.660
the therapies we can
prescribe, our understanding

00:52:34.660 --> 00:52:39.850
of the anatomy, physiology,
pathology, pathophysiology

00:52:39.850 --> 00:52:41.920
of human health
and disease, we've

00:52:41.920 --> 00:52:44.890
never risen to this level.

00:52:44.890 --> 00:52:46.820
But at the very
same moment, we're

00:52:46.820 --> 00:52:52.630
at perhaps unprecedented risk
of losing the human thread

00:52:52.630 --> 00:52:55.780
in medicine, attending
to things that

00:52:55.780 --> 00:52:59.990
are secondary at the expense
of those that are primary.

00:52:59.990 --> 00:53:04.060
So I would encourage you, if
you're contemplating a career

00:53:04.060 --> 00:53:08.530
in the health professions, to
get as good a liberal education

00:53:08.530 --> 00:53:09.730
as you can.

00:53:09.730 --> 00:53:15.520
And then bring that education
as a form of advocacy

00:53:15.520 --> 00:53:18.160
beginning day one
of medical school

00:53:18.160 --> 00:53:21.940
and carrying right through your
undergraduate medical education

00:53:21.940 --> 00:53:24.340
and into residency
and fellowship.

00:53:24.340 --> 00:53:27.070
We desperately
need people who can

00:53:27.070 --> 00:53:29.830
see what matters
most, who realize

00:53:29.830 --> 00:53:34.210
that not everything that can
be counted really counts,

00:53:34.210 --> 00:53:36.490
and who realize that
some of the things we

00:53:36.490 --> 00:53:41.260
spend a lot of time counting
don't really count for much.

00:53:41.260 --> 00:53:44.680
So never, at least
in my lifetime,

00:53:44.680 --> 00:53:48.160
has the need for liberally
educated people in the health

00:53:48.160 --> 00:53:49.830
professions been greater.

00:53:49.830 --> 00:53:54.040
So that's, in a way, a call
to arms, or at least a call

00:53:54.040 --> 00:53:55.820
to action.

00:53:55.820 --> 00:54:00.430
You can actually not only
care for your patients,

00:54:00.430 --> 00:54:04.960
but you can diagnose
and administer therapy

00:54:04.960 --> 00:54:07.780
to the profession
of medicine itself,

00:54:07.780 --> 00:54:11.950
which is not in the best
of health in some respects.

00:54:11.950 --> 00:54:16.300
But by showing us what medicine
looks like at its best,

00:54:16.300 --> 00:54:21.593
you can help us reconnect with
the core of medical excellence.

00:54:21.593 --> 00:54:22.885
Thanks again for your question.

00:54:26.690 --> 00:54:27.610
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:54:27.610 --> 00:54:29.740
Thank you so much for
coming out to speak to us.

00:54:29.740 --> 00:54:32.830
For someone who is planning
to go into the medical field

00:54:32.830 --> 00:54:35.470
and dealing with patients no
matter what-- whether it's

00:54:35.470 --> 00:54:37.870
psychological, whether
it's physical-- how would

00:54:37.870 --> 00:54:40.090
you suggest that they
best prepare themselves

00:54:40.090 --> 00:54:44.110
to face every patient with
the same intentionality

00:54:44.110 --> 00:54:46.120
and care as they
did the last one,

00:54:46.120 --> 00:54:48.910
as it can get kind of exhausting
to hear again and again,

00:54:48.910 --> 00:54:51.322
and you're bearing
someone else's burdens.

00:54:51.322 --> 00:54:53.530
RICHARD B. GUNDERMAN: Now,
that's a terrific problem.

00:54:53.530 --> 00:54:56.800
Physicians and health
professionals, in general,

00:54:56.800 --> 00:54:57.730
can burn out.

00:54:57.730 --> 00:55:01.240
You can only work so many
hours a day, only work

00:55:01.240 --> 00:55:02.960
at a certain pace.

00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:06.460
But I think one
reason that we face

00:55:06.460 --> 00:55:08.680
a challenge of
burnout in health care

00:55:08.680 --> 00:55:13.300
today is because sometimes
we are encouraged or perhaps

00:55:13.300 --> 00:55:17.500
forced to practice in
ways that prevent us

00:55:17.500 --> 00:55:20.980
from caring for patients
to the best of our ability.

00:55:20.980 --> 00:55:24.610
One of the most demoralizing
things that can happen to you

00:55:24.610 --> 00:55:28.690
is to have a job you care about
deeply and then be deprived

00:55:28.690 --> 00:55:31.030
of the opportunity
to do it well.

00:55:31.030 --> 00:55:35.140
So I don't claim to have
a panacea, a cure-all

00:55:35.140 --> 00:55:37.240
that we can administer
in this case.

00:55:37.240 --> 00:55:41.380
But I can imagine somebody,
before they enter the room

00:55:41.380 --> 00:55:46.690
with a patient, pausing
for a few seconds,

00:55:46.690 --> 00:55:51.960
perhaps to offer a prayer,
perhaps to reorient

00:55:51.960 --> 00:55:55.770
and recalibrate to what
this is really all about.

00:55:55.770 --> 00:55:58.050
In the hustle and
bustle, sometimes we

00:55:58.050 --> 00:55:59.650
can get disoriented.

00:55:59.650 --> 00:56:03.660
I think that disorientation
is a big part

00:56:03.660 --> 00:56:07.110
of people getting discouraged
and exhausted in medicine.

00:56:07.110 --> 00:56:09.410
And you can be
one of the people.

00:56:09.410 --> 00:56:10.980
None of us is perfect.

00:56:10.980 --> 00:56:12.960
There's no foolproof solution.

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:14.730
But you can be one
of the people who

00:56:14.730 --> 00:56:19.700
pauses to recall what
this is really all

00:56:19.700 --> 00:56:22.470
about before every
patient encounter.

00:56:22.470 --> 00:56:25.790
And I firmly believe that
can make a real difference,

00:56:25.790 --> 00:56:28.970
not only over the course
of a day of practice,

00:56:28.970 --> 00:56:33.270
but over the course of
a career of practice.

00:56:33.270 --> 00:56:34.320
Thank you.

00:56:34.320 --> 00:56:36.770
ANNA KATE HICKS: We have
time for one more question.

00:56:36.770 --> 00:56:39.470
AUDIENCE: Thank you
so much for your talk.

00:56:39.470 --> 00:56:43.130
So during this time
of COVID, there

00:56:43.130 --> 00:56:47.210
is a greater emphasis on
separating the elderly

00:56:47.210 --> 00:56:50.450
from the outside
for their protection

00:56:50.450 --> 00:56:53.030
and for their safety.

00:56:53.030 --> 00:56:56.750
Obviously, this contributes to
a greater amount of loneliness.

00:56:56.750 --> 00:57:03.050
Is there a relationship between
the sickness and loneliness?

00:57:03.050 --> 00:57:06.230
Are we doing more harm
than good by separating

00:57:06.230 --> 00:57:11.120
the elderly from contact with
their loved ones or family?

00:57:11.120 --> 00:57:13.370
RICHARD B. GUNDERMAN:
It's a great question.

00:57:13.370 --> 00:57:18.080
I may have this slightly wrong,
but former US Surgeon General

00:57:18.080 --> 00:57:22.910
Vivek Murthy published
a book, I think in 2018,

00:57:22.910 --> 00:57:25.400
called The Epidemic
of Loneliness.

00:57:25.400 --> 00:57:29.990
He basically presented
evidence that being lonely

00:57:29.990 --> 00:57:32.510
may be about as
bad for your health

00:57:32.510 --> 00:57:35.060
as smoking 15 cigarettes a day.

00:57:35.060 --> 00:57:40.130
That's a pretty serious
insult to your health.

00:57:40.130 --> 00:57:45.320
I do not think that captures
the damage done by loneliness.

00:57:45.320 --> 00:57:49.190
To say it decreases
our life expectancy

00:57:49.190 --> 00:57:54.110
or increases the number
of days lost from work

00:57:54.110 --> 00:57:57.650
only scratches the surface
of a much deeper reality.

00:57:57.650 --> 00:58:00.920
But there is a recognition
that loneliness

00:58:00.920 --> 00:58:03.350
is bad for human beings.

00:58:03.350 --> 00:58:06.470
After the World Wars
there were large numbers

00:58:06.470 --> 00:58:10.730
of orphans in Europe, and they
were placed in institutions.

00:58:10.730 --> 00:58:13.010
You know, their
diapers were changed.

00:58:13.010 --> 00:58:15.170
They were fed regularly.

00:58:15.170 --> 00:58:17.900
The ambient
temperature was right.

00:58:17.900 --> 00:58:21.140
But in many cases, they not
only didn't grow and develop

00:58:21.140 --> 00:58:24.110
normally, but actually
withered away.

00:58:24.110 --> 00:58:28.820
And for a time, that syndrome
was referred to as hospitalism.

00:58:28.820 --> 00:58:32.090
Today we call it
failure to thrive.

00:58:32.090 --> 00:58:34.910
But the lesson there is
we human beings don't just

00:58:34.910 --> 00:58:39.980
need nutrients, adequate
hydration, sanitation

00:58:39.980 --> 00:58:41.660
or hygiene.

00:58:41.660 --> 00:58:48.130
We also need human contact,
by which I mean in part touch.

00:58:48.130 --> 00:58:52.030
We human beings need to
touch and be touched.

00:58:52.030 --> 00:58:55.990
And, because of COVID, there
are nursing home residents

00:58:55.990 --> 00:58:58.120
around the country
who at points have

00:58:58.120 --> 00:59:01.120
been confined to their
rooms for periods of time

00:59:01.120 --> 00:59:02.950
as long as months.

00:59:02.950 --> 00:59:07.060
And no visitors were
allowed in the facility.

00:59:07.060 --> 00:59:09.340
We can do some things
with tablet computers,

00:59:09.340 --> 00:59:14.680
[LAUGHS] various video
conferencing software.

00:59:14.680 --> 00:59:19.120
But I think there's, in the end,
no substitute for human touch.

00:59:19.120 --> 00:59:22.420
And I don't pretend to
have a simple solution.

00:59:22.420 --> 00:59:25.480
But we must make sure when
we're thinking about things

00:59:25.480 --> 00:59:30.730
like death rates, number of
years of lives lost, that we're

00:59:30.730 --> 00:59:34.600
also thinking about
what life really means,

00:59:34.600 --> 00:59:36.970
what it takes to
come fully to life,

00:59:36.970 --> 00:59:40.340
at least to the extent
we can as human beings.

00:59:40.340 --> 00:59:43.510
And I think part of
that is human contact.

00:59:43.510 --> 00:59:47.620
And that's actually a role that
you could play to some degree.

00:59:47.620 --> 00:59:51.940
You may know somebody in your
family, from your neighborhood,

00:59:51.940 --> 00:59:53.830
perhaps here in
this town, perhaps

00:59:53.830 --> 00:59:56.020
on this campus, who's lonely.

00:59:56.020 --> 00:59:59.860
And each of us has
it within our power

00:59:59.860 --> 01:00:02.860
to reach out to one
such person and let

01:00:02.860 --> 01:00:05.290
them know we're thinking
about them, maybe

01:00:05.290 --> 01:00:08.680
even to schedule a
conversation, if possible,

01:00:08.680 --> 01:00:10.030
perhaps even a visit.

01:00:10.030 --> 01:00:16.560
You do not need a
bachelor's degree or an MD

01:00:16.560 --> 01:00:23.130
Or a residency certification to
reach out to people to let them

01:00:23.130 --> 01:00:26.550
know you're thinking
about them, to let them

01:00:26.550 --> 01:00:28.440
know that you care about them.

01:00:28.440 --> 01:00:31.470
That's within the
range of competence

01:00:31.470 --> 01:00:34.560
of every single person
in this theater.

01:00:34.560 --> 01:00:38.040
And it's a responsibility
and an opportunity

01:00:38.040 --> 01:00:41.250
I would encourage you
to take seriously.

01:00:41.250 --> 01:00:43.590
If you start doing that now--

01:00:43.590 --> 01:00:47.550
if you go to nursing school or
dental school or medical school

01:00:47.550 --> 01:00:52.180
or what have you, you will
bring much better habits--

01:00:52.180 --> 01:00:56.200
habits of perception,
of understanding,

01:00:56.200 --> 01:00:57.910
habits of the heart--

01:00:57.910 --> 01:01:03.970
than some of the rest of us
did when we buffed up our GPAs

01:01:03.970 --> 01:01:08.350
and maxed out our medical
college admission test scores.

01:01:08.350 --> 01:01:12.130
Those things are good, but
they're not what matters most.

01:01:12.130 --> 01:01:13.630
And if you're one
of the people who

01:01:13.630 --> 01:01:18.910
sees what matters most and has
developed habits of reaching

01:01:18.910 --> 01:01:22.720
out to people to tend
what matters most,

01:01:22.720 --> 01:01:26.110
you can make very
significant contributions

01:01:26.110 --> 01:01:28.923
to the healing professions.

01:01:28.923 --> 01:01:31.340
ANNA KATE HICKS: Please join
me in thanking Dr. Gunderman.

01:01:31.340 --> 01:01:34.690
[APPLAUSE]

